rule apparel_scope with provenance
        because "the model covers upper-body garments: headings 6105, 6106, 6109, 6110, 6205 and 6206":
a good is within the scope of the model
    if the good is an upper body garment.

% GRI 6 and Section XI subheading note 2: the subheading of the heading, for
% the textile material of what the garment is classified as (GRI 3(b)).
rule apparel_subheading with provenance as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50
        at "Products of chapters 56 to 63 containing two or more textile materials are to be regarded as consisting wholly of that textile material which would be selected under note 2 to this section",
        because "Section XI subheading note 2(A), read with GRI 6":
the subheading of a good is a code
    if the heading of the good is a heading
    and the good is classified as consisting of a component
    and the textile material of the component is a group
    and the subheading for the group in heading the heading is the code under table apparel.

rule apparel_construction with provenance as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50
        at "only the part which determines the classification under general interpretative rule 3 shall be taken into account",
        because "Section XI subheading note 2(B)(a): a garment's fabric is that of the part that gives it its essential character":
the construction of a good is a construction
    if the good is classified as consisting of a component
    and the fabric construction of the component is the construction.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Section XI note 2(A): a good of two or more textile materials is classified
% as if it consisted wholly of the one that predominates by weight over each
% other single textile material; when none predominates, as if it consisted of
% the one of the heading that occurs last in numerical order. Note 2(B)(b):
% first the chapter, then the heading within it — so the man-made fibres of
% Chapters 54 and 55 count together (2(B)(c)). Note 2(B)(a): a metalized yarn
% (heading 5605) is one textile material, whatever it is made of.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

cotton belongs to cotton, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 52 at "CHAPTER 52 COTTON".
polyester belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By polymerization of organic monomers to produce polymers such as polyamides, polyesters, polyolefins or polyurethanes".
elastane belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By polymerization of organic monomers to produce polymers such as polyamides, polyesters, polyolefins or polyurethanes",
    because "elastane is a segmented polyurethane".
spandex belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By polymerization of organic monomers to produce polymers such as polyamides, polyesters, polyolefins or polyurethanes",
    because "spandex is the American name of elastane, a segmented polyurethane".
nylon belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By polymerization of organic monomers to produce polymers such as polyamides, polyesters, polyolefins or polyurethanes",
    because "nylon is a polyamide".
polyamide belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By polymerization of organic monomers to produce polymers such as polyamides, polyesters, polyolefins or polyurethanes".
acrylic belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By polymerization of organic monomers",
    because "acrylic fibres are polymers of acrylonitrile".
rayon belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By dissolution or chemical treatment of natural organic polymers (for example, cellulose) to produce polymers such as cuprammonium rayon (cupro) or viscose rayon".
viscose belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By dissolution or chemical treatment of natural organic polymers (for example, cellulose) to produce polymers such as cuprammonium rayon (cupro) or viscose rayon".
modal belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By dissolution or chemical treatment of natural organic polymers (for example, cellulose)",
    because "modal is a regenerated cellulose fibre".
lyocell belongs to man-made fibres,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 54 at "By dissolution or chemical treatment of natural organic polymers (for example, cellulose)",
    because "lyocell is a regenerated cellulose fibre".
wool belongs to wool fibres, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 51 at "means the natural fiber grown by sheep or lambs".
cashmere belongs to wool fibres, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 51 at "Tibetan, Kashmir or similar goats".
silk belongs to silk, as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50 at "CHAPTER 50 SILK".
flax belongs to vegetable fibres, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 53 at "Flax, raw or processed but not spun".
linen belongs to vegetable fibres, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 53 at "Flax, raw or processed but not spun",
    because "linen is flax".
metalized yarn belongs to metalized yarn,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 56 at "Metalized yarn, whether or not gimped, being textile yarn".

silk is a textile group of chapter 50, as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50 at "CHAPTER 50 SILK".
wool fibres is a textile group of chapter 51, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 51 at "CHAPTER 51 WOOL, FINE OR COARSE ANIMAL HAIR".
cotton is a textile group of chapter 52, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 52 at "CHAPTER 52 COTTON".
vegetable fibres is a textile group of chapter 53, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 53 at "CHAPTER 53 OTHER VEGETABLE TEXTILE FIBERS".
man-made fibres is a textile group of chapter 55,
    as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50 at "When both chapters 54 and 55 are involved with any other chapter, chapters 54 and 55 are to be treated as a single chapter",
    because "counted as one chapter, numbered by the later of the two (55) when the last heading decides a tie".
metalized yarn is a textile group of chapter 56,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 56 at "CHAPTER 56 WADDING, FELT AND NONWOVENS; SPECIAL YARNS".

rule section_xi_2b_share with provenance as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50
        at "The choice of appropriate heading shall be effected by determining first the chapter and then the applicable heading within that chapter":
the share of a group in a garment is a total
    if the group is a textile group of chapter a number
    and the total is the sum of each percentage such that
        the garment contains the percentage percent of a material
        and the material belongs to the group.

rule section_xi_2ba_metalized with provenance as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50
        at "metalized yarn (heading 5605) are to be treated as a single textile material the weight of which is to be taken as the aggregate of the weights of its components":
the textile material of a garment is metalized yarn
    if all the yarn of the garment is metalized yarn.

rule section_xi_2a_predominance with provenance as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50
        at "are to be classified as if consisting wholly of that one textile material which predominates by weight over each other single textile material":
the textile material of a garment is a group
    if it is not the case that
        all the yarn of the garment is metalized yarn
    and the share of the group in the garment is a total
    and the total > 0
    and for all cases in which
            the share of an other group in the garment is an other total
            and it is not the case that
                the other group is equal to the group
        it is the case that
            the group at the total percent outweighs the other group at the other total percent.

rule section_xi_2a_weight with provenance as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50
        at "which predominates by weight over each other single textile material":
a group at a total percent outweighs an other group at an other total percent
    if the total > the other total.

rule section_xi_2a_tie with provenance as stated in HTSUS Section XI and Chapter 50
        at "When no one textile material predominates by weight, the goods are to be classified as if consisting wholly of that one textile material which is covered by the heading which occurs last in numerical order":
a group at a total percent outweighs an other group at an other total percent
    if the total = the other total
    and the group is a textile group of chapter a number
    and the other group is a textile group of chapter an other number
    and the number > the other number.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Chapter 61 note 9, Chapter 62 note 9: left over right closure is men's,
% right over left women's, unless the cut clearly indicates the sex; a garment
% that cannot be identified goes to the women's headings.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule note_61_9_cut with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "the cut of the garment clearly indicates that it is designed for one or other of the sexes",
        because "Chapter 61 note 9 and Chapter 62 note 9: the closure rule does not apply when the cut shows the sex":
the gender of a garment is a gender
    if the cut of the garment indicates the gender.

rule note_61_9_left_over_right with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "Garments of this chapter designed for left over right closure at the front shall be regarded as men's or boys' garments":
the gender of a garment is men
    if it is not the case that
        the cut of the garment indicates a gender
    and the garment closes left over right.

rule note_61_9_right_over_left with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "those designed for right over left closure at the front as women's or girls' garments":
the gender of a garment is women
    if it is not the case that
        the cut of the garment indicates a gender
    and the garment closes right over left.

rule note_61_9_unidentified with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "Garments which cannot be identified as either men's or boys' garments or as women's or girls' garments are to be classified in the headings covering women's or girls' garments":
the gender of a garment is women
    if it is not the case that
        the cut of the garment indicates a gender
    and it is not the case that
        the garment closes left over right
    and it is not the case that
        the garment closes right over left.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Chapter 61 note 4, Chapter 62 note 4: "Shirts" and "shirt-blouses" have long
% or short sleeves and a full or partial opening starting at the neckline;
% "blouses" are loose-fitting, may be sleeveless, with or without an opening.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule note_61_4_shirts with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "are garments designed to cover the upper part of the body, having long or short sleeves and a full or partial opening starting at the neckline":
a garment is a shirt
    if it is not the case that
        the garment is sleeveless
    and the garment has an opening starting at the neckline.

rule note_61_4_blouses with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "are loose-fitting garments also designed to cover the upper part of the body but may be sleeveless and with or without an opening at the neckline":
a garment is a blouse
    if the garment is loose-fitting.

rule note_61_4_blouse_type with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "may also have a collar",
        because "Note 4 defines shirts, shirt-blouses and blouses together; heading 6106 and 6206 cover all three":
a garment is a blouse-type garment
    if the garment is a shirt
    otherwise the garment is a blouse.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Heading 6109. The Explanatory Note: T-shirts are lightweight knitted
% garments, not napped nor of terry, with long or short sleeves, without
% collar, without opening or fastening at the neckline, with a close-fitting or
% lower neckline; the heading also covers singlets and other vests. CBP adds,
% since HQ 950855 (1992), that the fabric be plain jersey or plain interlock.
% A tank top, for CBP, has the tank silhouette: straps, and a rear neckline
% below the nape of the neck (NY N360982).
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule en_6109_tshirt_fabric with provenance as stated in ruling NY N362034
        at "To be classifiable as a T-shirt, a garment must be constructed from lightweight, plain jersey or plain interlock fabric",
        because "the Explanatory Note to heading 6109 describes T-shirts as lightweight knitted garments, not napped nor of terry":
a garment has the fabric of a T-shirt
    if the fabric of the garment is lightweight
    and it is not the case that
        the garment is napped
    and it is not the case that
        the fabric of the garment is terry
    and the garment meets the knit test of its tariff for T-shirts.

rule cbp_hq_950855 with provenance as stated in ruling NY N362034
        at "plain jersey or plain interlock fabric; see Headquarters Ruling HQ 950855":
a garment meets the knit test of its tariff for T-shirts
    if the garment is classified under the US tariff
    and the garment is of plain knit fabric.

rule en_6109_eu_fabric with provenance
        because "EU offices apply the Explanatory Note to heading 6109 without CBP's plain jersey or interlock test":
a garment meets the knit test of its tariff for T-shirts
    if the garment is classified under the EU tariff.

rule en_6109_tshirt with provenance as stated in BTI FRBTIFR-BTI-2025-07379
        at "les vêtements repris à cette position ne doivent pas comporter de dentelles, d'ouverture à l'encolure ou de bouton ou autre système de fermeture",
        because "the Explanatory Note to heading 6109, as a French BTI quotes it: no opening or fastening at the neckline; the Note also excludes collars and has sleeves":
a garment is a T-shirt
    if it is not the case that
        the garment is sleeveless
    and the garment has the fabric of a T-shirt
    and it is not the case that
        the garment has a collar
    and it is not the case that
        the garment has a high neckline
    and it is not the case that
        the garment has an opening starting at the neckline.

rule cbp_tank_silhouette with provenance as stated in ruling NY N360982
        at "it lacks a tank silhouette. Specifically, as noted above, the rear neckline does not fall below the nape of the neck, and there is no appearance of straps in the back":
a garment has a tank silhouette
    if the garment has shoulder straps
    and the rear neckline of the garment falls below the nape of the neck.

rule cbp_tank_top with provenance as stated in ruling NY N360982
        at "Although you refer to the garment as a tank top, it lacks a tank silhouette":
a garment is a tank top
    if the garment is sleeveless
    and the garment has a tank silhouette.

rule heading_6109_singlets with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "T-shirts, singlets, tank tops and similar garments, knitted or crocheted",
        because "the Explanatory Note to heading 6109 includes singlets and other vests: garments worn as undergarments":
a garment is a singlet
    if the garment is designed to be worn as an undergarment.

rule heading_6109_family with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "T-shirts, singlets, tank tops and similar garments, knitted or crocheted":
a garment is of the T-shirt family
    if the garment is a T-shirt
    otherwise the garment is a tank top
    otherwise the garment is a singlet.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The headings (GRI 1). Chapter 61 note 1: only knitted articles; Chapter 62
% note 1: only articles that are not knitted.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule heading_6105 with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "Men's or boys' shirts, knitted or crocheted",
        because "Chapter 61 note 1: only knitted or crocheted articles":
a garment is described by heading 6105
    if the construction of the garment is knitted
    and the gender of the garment is men
    and the garment is a shirt.

rule heading_6106 with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "Women's or girls' blouses and shirts, knitted or crocheted":
a garment is described by heading 6106
    if the construction of the garment is knitted
    and the gender of the garment is women
    and the garment is a blouse-type garment.

rule heading_6109 with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "T-shirts, singlets, tank tops and similar garments, knitted or crocheted":
a garment is described by heading 6109
    if the construction of the garment is knitted
    and the garment is of the T-shirt family.

% The Explanatory Note to heading 6110: a category of knitted articles, without
% distinction as to sex, designed to cover the upper parts of the body.
rule heading_6110 with provenance as stated in BTI FRBTIFR-BTI-2025-06897
        at "la présente position couvre une catégorie d'articles en bonneterie, sans distinction quant au sexe, destinés à recouvrir la partie supérieure du corps",
        because "the Explanatory Note to heading 6110 (Sweaters, pullovers, sweatshirts, waistcoats and similar articles, knitted or crocheted), as a French BTI quotes it":
a garment is described by heading 6110
    if the construction of the garment is knitted.

rule heading_6205 with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 62
        at "Men's or boys' shirts",
        because "Chapter 62 note 1: articles other than knitted or crocheted":
a garment is described by heading 6205
    if the construction of the garment is woven
    and the gender of the garment is men
    and the garment is a shirt.

rule heading_6206 with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 62
        at "Women's or girls' blouses, shirts and shirt-blouses":
a garment is described by heading 6206
    if the construction of the garment is woven
    and the gender of the garment is women
    and the garment is a blouse-type garment.

% The exclusions. Chapter 61 note 4 and Chapter 62 note 4: the shirt headings
% do not cover garments with pockets below the waist, or with a ribbed
% waistband or other means of tightening at the bottom; the knitted ones not
% those with less than 10 stitches per linear centimetre in each direction;
% 6105 and 6205 not sleeveless garments. Chapter 61 note 5: 6109 does not
% cover garments with a drawstring, ribbed waistband or other means of
% tightening at the bottom.

heading 6105 is a shirt heading, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61 at "Headings 6105 and 6106 do not cover garments with pockets below the waist".
heading 6106 is a shirt heading, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61 at "Headings 6105 and 6106 do not cover garments with pockets below the waist".
heading 6205 is a shirt heading, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 62 at "Headings 6205 and 6206 do not cover garments with pockets below the waist".
heading 6206 is a shirt heading, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 62 at "Headings 6205 and 6206 do not cover garments with pockets below the waist".
heading 6105 is a knitted shirt heading,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61 at "garments having an average of less than 10 stiches per linear centimeter in each direction".
heading 6106 is a knitted shirt heading,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61 at "garments having an average of less than 10 stiches per linear centimeter in each direction".
heading 6105 excludes sleeveless garments, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61 at "Heading 6105 does not cover sleeveless garments".
heading 6205 excludes sleeveless garments, as stated in HTSUS Chapter 62 at "Heading 6205 does not cover sleeveless garments".

rule note_61_4_pockets with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "Headings 6105 and 6106 do not cover garments with pockets below the waist",
        because "the same in Chapter 62 note 4 for headings 6205 and 6206":
a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading is a shirt heading
    and the garment has pockets below the waist.

rule note_61_4_tightening with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "with a ribbed waistband or other means of tightening at the bottom of the garment",
        because "the same in Chapter 62 note 4 for headings 6205 and 6206":
a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading is a shirt heading
    and the garment has a means of tightening at the bottom.

rule note_61_4_stitches with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "garments having an average of less than 10 stiches per linear centimeter in each direction counted on an area measuring at least 10 cm by 10 cm":
a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading is a knitted shirt heading
    and the garment has less than ten stitches per centimetre in each direction.

rule note_61_4_sleeveless with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "Heading 6105 does not cover sleeveless garments",
        because "the same in Chapter 62 note 4 for heading 6205":
a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading excludes sleeveless garments
    and the garment is sleeveless.

rule note_61_5 with provenance as stated in HTSUS Chapter 61
        at "Heading 6109 does not cover garments with a drawstring, ribbed waistband or other means of tightening at the bottom of the garment":
a garment is excluded from heading 6109
    if the garment has a means of tightening at the bottom.

% GRI 3(a): the more specific description. Heading 6110 names a category
% ("... and similar articles") of which shirts, blouses and T-shirts are
% particular kinds; and CBP holds a loose-fitting women's top to be a blouse
% rather than a T-shirt.
heading 6105 is more specific than heading 6110,
    as stated in HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation at "The heading which provides the most specific description shall be preferred to headings providing a more general description",
    because "a shirt is a particular kind of the upper-body garments of heading 6110".
heading 6106 is more specific than heading 6110,
    as stated in HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation at "The heading which provides the most specific description shall be preferred to headings providing a more general description",
    because "a blouse or shirt is a particular kind of the upper-body garments of heading 6110".
heading 6109 is more specific than heading 6110,
    as stated in HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation at "The heading which provides the most specific description shall be preferred to headings providing a more general description",
    because "a T-shirt, tank top or singlet is a particular kind of the upper-body garments of heading 6110".
heading 6106 is more specific than heading 6109,
    according to CBP, as stated in ruling NY N356079
        at "in accordance with the reasoning set forth in Headquarters Ruling Letters H325360 and H324935",
    because "loose-fitting women's tops have the characteristics of blouses of Note 4 to Chapter 61, which precludes headings 6109 and 6110".
